## Quick start

```bash
make bootstrap   # first-time setup
make dev         # start the local stack
make health      # verify all services, and print their URLs
make down        # stop containers
make ports       # re-resolve host ports
```

### Host ports are resolved, not fixed

A host port is a machine-global resource, so this project does not claim fixed
ones. `make bootstrap` runs `local/scripts/ports.sh`, which keeps each preferred
port when it is free and takes the next free one when it is not, recording the
result in `local/.env` — read by Docker Compose, the Caddyfile, and the app dev
servers alike. The file is machine-specific and is not committed.

Assignments are reused once made, so URLs stay stable across restarts. Run
`make ports` to re-resolve after freeing a port you would rather have. Because
the proxy does not bind 443, application URLs carry its HTTPS port; `make health`
prints the current ones.

Run `make profile` to print the profile and enabled components at any time.

### On Windows, run the scripts under Git Bash

`bash` from PowerShell is `C:\WINDOWS\system32\bash.exe` — the WSL launcher.
WSL is a separate Linux filesystem and cannot see a winget or Scoop install on
the Windows side, so `local/scripts/*.sh` will report that `doppler`,
`terraform` or `python` "is not installed" when it is. Either use a Git Bash
terminal, name it explicitly:

```bash
"C:/Program Files/Git/bin/bash.exe" local/scripts/doppler-bootstrap.sh
```

or add this to your PowerShell profile, which leaves WSL reachable as `wsl`:

```powershell
function bash { & 'C:\Program Files\Git\bin\bash.exe' @args }
```

`make` targets are unaffected — make resolves `bash` itself and finds Git Bash.

**Without `make`** (Windows without Git Bash's make, or a minimal container),
the same steps are pnpm scripts:

```bash
pnpm bootstrap      # = make bootstrap
pnpm stack:up       # = make dev   (containers only; `pnpm dev` runs the apps)
pnpm stack:health   # = make health
pnpm stack:preflight # = make preflight (read-only: ports, placeholder secrets, missing config)
pnpm stack:stop     # = make stop  (stops containers; keeps volumes)
pnpm stack:down     # = make down  (removes containers; keeps volumes)
pnpm stack:status   # = make zitadel-status
pnpm stack:ports    # = make ports
pnpm stack:reset    # = make reset -- DESTRUCTIVE: deletes this stack's volumes,
                    #   including the local ZITADEL instance. Guarded: refuses in a
                    #   worktree or against non-local endpoints, and asks first.
```

### Local identity (ZITADEL)

The local ZITADEL instance is created by `make bootstrap` and started by
`make dev`, both through `local/scripts/stack.mjs`; a bare
`docker compose up` refuses on purpose. Each instance gets a masterkey and an
admin password generated on this machine, kept owner-only under
`~/.koras/secrets/`, and its record is `~/.koras/state/{{projectSlug}}/zitadel.json`.

Before the first bootstrap the key must be escrowable twice -- ADR 0015 in the
starter explains why -- so three things must exist, all set up by the owner:

- a Doppler branch config `dev_local_<machine>` under `dev` in project
  `{{projectSlug}}` (the machine id is in `~/.koras/machine.json`);
- `~/.koras/recovery-recipient.json`, the pinned public recovery key;
- `KORAS_ESCROW_BACKUP_DIR`, a directory on a different drive from your home.

Without them bootstrap stops before generating anything. `KORAS_ZITADEL_OFFLINE=1
make bootstrap` creates an unescrowed instance instead, which nothing starts
until `make zitadel-escrow` completes both legs.

```bash
make zitadel-status                               # mode, escrow, token expiry
node local/scripts/stack.mjs status --show-admin  # the admin password, on request
make zitadel-rotate-pat                           # before the tokens expire (365 days)
node local/scripts/stack.mjs recover              # a lost key cache, from escrow
```

Sign-in runs on ZITADEL's login v2 at
`http://localhost:${KORAS_PORT_ZITADEL_LOGIN}/ui/v2/login/`, a port of its own.

